"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The positive surgical findings include a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5, positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, and the presence of lymph node metastasis. Countervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion. The preoperative PSA was 10. The biopsy demonstrated a Gleason 3+4 pattern, which differs from the prostatectomy specimen. The presence of lymph node metastasis is a significant negative prognostic factor. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. The current horizon suggests a time frame for recurrence. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trend would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 39.80 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 29.85–49.75 months; its coverage has not been validated."
